          Weeds in driveway cracks are a two-part problem: the plant, and the gap full of grit and moisture it grows in. Any method will clear the plant for a while. Pulling and boiling water are free, flame and 20% vinegar burn the tops fast, a glyphosate product kills the roots, and residual products like Ortho GroundClear Year Long or Roundup Max Control 365 keep bare ground bare for up to a year. None of them is permanent on its own. Kill what is there, dig out the roots and debris, then fill and seal the crack so nothing has anywhere to grow.
        </p>

        <div id="why-cracks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Weeds Love Driveway Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            A driveway crack is a surprisingly good place to be a weed. Wind and rain wash fine soil, sand and leaf debris into the gap, and it settles into a narrow strip of growing medium. The slab on either side shades the soil, slows evaporation and holds moisture underneath, so the crack stays damp long after the lawn has dried out. Seeds blow in from the yard, lodge in the debris, and sprout with almost no competition. Annual grasses, crabgrass, spurge, chickweed and plantain show up first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Perennials are the real nuisance. Dandelions drive a taproot down past the slab. Bindweed, quackgrass and other creeping weeds run roots and rhizomes under the pavement and send shoots up through every crack along the way, which is why the same line of weeds reappears no matter how often you pull the tops. If the weeds in your cracks come back within two weeks of any treatment, assume you are dealing with something rooted under the driveway, not just in the crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weeds are not just cosmetic. Roots open the crack a little wider each season, organic matter holds water in it, and in cold climates that water freezes, expands and breaks off the edges. A hairline crack with weeds in it becomes a wide crack with weeds in it within a few years. That is why every method below ends at the same place: once the weed is dead, the crack has to be cleaned out and closed, or you are simply scheduling the next round.
          </p>

          <p>
            The right approach also depends on the surface. Asphalt, concrete and pavers crack differently, tolerate different chemicals differently, and get sealed with different products. We cover the differences in section six. If you just want the short version: kill the weeds with whatever suits your tolerance for chemicals, then fill asphalt with a rubberized crack filler, concrete with a flexible sealant, and paver joints with polymeric sand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pull" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pull and Scrape: The Free Method
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hand removal costs nothing and works better than its reputation, as long as you pick your moment. Pull the day after a soaking rain or a deep watering, when the soil in the crack is soft and roots slide out instead of snapping. A narrow crack weeder, an old flat screwdriver or a stiff putty knife gets under the crown and levers the root out. For a long run of cracks, a long-handled crack weeding tool saves your back and gets surprisingly good leverage on established clumps.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is the root, not the leaves. With dandelions and other taprooted weeds, work the tool down beside the root and lift slowly. If the taproot snaps a few inches down, it will regrow, though each regrowth weakens it. With creeping weeds like bindweed and quackgrass, hand removal mostly controls the visible growth, because the root system extends well under the slab where no tool can reach. Pulling is excellent for annuals and a maintenance routine for perennials, not a cure.
          </p>

          <p>
            After pulling, scrape out the debris that let the weed grow. A wire brush, the hooked end of a crack weeder or a shop vac pulls out the silt and dead plant matter. A pressure washer clears debris fast from concrete cracks, but use a fan tip and keep your distance on asphalt, which can be gouged, and on pavers, where it will blast out the joint sand. Removing that debris also removes the seeds sitting in it, which is half the reason the crack grows weeds at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest verdict: if you have a modest driveway and a few dozen weeds, an hour with a crack weeder after rain is the cheapest, safest and most thorough first step. It leaves no residue, it does not harm anything nearby, and it gives you a clean crack ready to fill. If you have a long gravel-edged drive with perennial weeds coming up every few inches, you will want one of the methods below as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Flame and Boiling Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat kills weeds by bursting their cells, and on hardscape it is one of the few methods with no chemical residue at all. A propane flame weeder is the faster option for long runs of cracks. The trick is that you are not trying to burn the weed to ash. A brief pass of a second or two is enough to make the cell sap expand and rupture the cell walls. The weed will not fall over immediately, but within hours to a couple of days it wilts and dies. Charring it black wastes fuel and time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Like vinegar, flame is a contact method. It kills annual weeds and seedlings outright, and it knocks perennials back without touching the roots. Extension guidance on flame weeding is consistent that perennials regrow after a few weeks and that repeat treatments through the season are needed to deplete their root reserves. Plan on a pass every few weeks during the growing season if your cracks hold perennial weeds, and expect the number of shoots to fall each time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fire safety is not optional. Never flame near dry grass, mulch, leaf litter, wooden fences, siding or a parked car, and never in drought conditions or where burn bans are in force. Keep a hose charged and within reach. Be careful with asphalt: a lingering flame softens the binder and can scorch the surface, so keep the torch moving. Flame weeders are best on concrete and pavers with green, not dry, weeds and a damp day helps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Boiling water is the kitchen version of the same idea. Iowa State University Extension notes that it acts as a contact treatment, killing only what it touches, and is most effective on young annual weeds, which makes cracks in patios and sidewalks one of the few places it is practical, since nothing desirable is nearby. Use plenty of water, pour slowly into the crack so it soaks the crown, and plan to repeat in seven to ten days on anything deep-rooted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Boiling water is free and residue-free, but it is slow for a whole driveway and carrying a full kettle around is a genuine scald risk. It suits a handful of cracks near the house, a front walk, or paver joints you do not want to put chemicals on. For more than that, a flame weeder covers ground far faster for the same effect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Vinegar and Contact Sprays
          </h2>

          <p>
            Contact sprays are the fast, satisfying option: spray in the morning, and the weeds are brown by afternoon. Horticultural vinegar at around 20% acetic acid works well on young annual weeds in cracks, especially in warm, sunny weather. Our full breakdown of <a href="/does-vinegar-kill-weeds/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether vinegar kills weeds</a> covers the strengths, the eye and skin hazard of the 20% products, and why kitchen 5% vinegar is only reliable on seedlings. On perennial crack weeds, vinegar burns the tops and the roots regrow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinegar has a specific downside on driveways. It is an acid, and concrete is alkaline. Acetic acid reacts with the cement paste, and repeated applications can dull, etch and roughen the surface, particularly on unsealed concrete. It will etch natural stone such as limestone, travertine and marble quickly. If you use vinegar on concrete or stone, spray the weed rather than the slab, rinse overspray off the surface with water, and do not make it a weekly habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Synthetic contact killers work the same way. Spectracide Weed and Grass Killer, which uses diquat, browns weeds within hours to a day and is non-selective, so it kills grass at the driveway edge just as readily. Like vinegar, it does not reach the roots, so it is a burn-down for annuals and a temporary setback for perennials. Its advantage is that it does not attack concrete the way acid does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip salt entirely on a driveway. It kills weeds, but it soaks into concrete and contributes to surface scaling, and it washes off the edge of the drive into the lawn, where it builds up in the soil and leaves a dead strip. Bleach is no better: it is not labeled as a weed killer, it runs off into the soil, and mixing it with vinegar releases chlorine gas. Neither is worth the damage to fix a cosmetic problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="systemic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Systemic and Residual Weed Killers
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the same weeds keep coming back, you need something that reaches the roots. Glyphosate is the standard systemic: the leaves absorb it and the plant carries it down into the root system over about a week, which is why it kills bindweed, quackgrass and established dandelions that every contact method only sets back. Results are slow, often 7 to 14 days before the weed is fully dead. Spray when the weeds are actively growing, on a dry, still day, and do not re-spray at day three because nothing seems to have happened.
          </p>

          <p>
            Residual vegetation killers go a step further. They combine a knockdown ingredient with one that stays in the soil and stops new seeds from establishing, which is the selling point for gravel drives and cracked pavement. We verified the actives on the EPA labels for the two best-known consumer products. Ortho GroundClear Year Long Vegetation Killer pairs glyphosate with imazapyr. Roundup Max Control 365 in its ready-to-use form combines glyphosate with imazapic and a small amount of diquat. Both are labeled to prevent regrowth for up to a year.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Active ingredients</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How it works</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lasts</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Roundup Weed and Grass Killer concentrates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glyphosate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Systemic, kills roots, no soil residual</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Until new seeds blow in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spectracide Weed and Grass Killer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diquat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contact, top growth only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Until roots regrow</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ortho GroundClear Year Long Vegetation Killer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glyphosate plus imazapyr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Systemic plus soil residual</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 12 months per label</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Roundup Max Control 365</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glyphosate, imazapic, diquat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Systemic plus soil residual</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 12 months per label</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The residual is also the risk. These products are meant for driveways, patios, sidewalks and gravel areas, and their labels warn against applying them where the roots of trees, shrubs or lawn may extend. Tree roots very often run under a driveway, and lawn grass reaches into the edge. The soil-active ingredient moves with water, so a slope that drains toward a bed, or rain soon after spraying, can carry it onto plants you want. Treat cracks on a dry forecast, keep away from the drip line of trees, and do not spray uphill of anything you care about.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the whole label before you buy, and follow it, because the label is the legal instruction for that product. Keep children and pets off until the spray has dried, and check any local rules, since some municipalities restrict residential herbicide use. Our <a href="/best-weed-killer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best weed killer guide</a> compares these products head to head, including the season-long gravel test that showed how much spraying a residual can save on a long driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Asphalt vs Concrete vs Pavers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each driveway surface fails a little differently, which changes both how weeds get in and how you shut them out. Treat the material first and the weeds second, and you end up doing far less weeding over the life of the driveway.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where weeds grow</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Seal with</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Random cracks, edges, alligator cracking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lingering flame, solvent sprays</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rubberized or asphalt emulsion crack filler, then sealcoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Control joints, random cracks, slab edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repeated acid, salt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible polyurethane or self-leveling crack sealant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every joint, especially low or washed-out ones</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pressure washing joints, acid on natural stone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymeric sand after cleaning joints to depth</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Asphalt is flexible and cracks as it ages, dries out and moves with the seasons. Weeds come up through random cracks and along the unsupported edges where the drive meets the lawn. Kill the weeds, clean and dry the crack, then fill it. Narrow cracks take a pourable rubberized filler, and wider ones need a thicker product or backing material; our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">driveway crack filler guide</a> matches products to crack widths. Shaded asphalt that grows moss in the cracks needs a different approach, covered in <a href="/how-to-remove-moss-from-asphalt-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to kill moss on an asphalt driveway</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete grows weeds mainly in its control joints, the grooves cut on purpose so the slab cracks along a line, and in random cracks from settling. Weeds in a control joint are a sign the joint was never sealed or the sealant has failed. Concrete moves less than asphalt, but it still expands and contracts, so use a flexible sealant rather than rigid patching mortar in joints, with foam backer rod in deeper gaps. Keep acid and salt off the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers have the most joints of all, and every one is a potential weed bed. Weeds root in joint sand that has washed low, and seeds land in the debris on top. Kill the weeds, then clean the joints out to the depth the sand maker specifies and refill. Regular sand will keep growing weeds; polymeric sand hardens into a firm joint that resists both weeds and washout. Our comparison of <a href="/polymeric-sand-vs-regular-sand-for-pavers/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">polymeric sand vs regular sand for pavers</a> explains when the upgrade is worth it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Prevention: Seal the Crack So Nothing Can Grow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every method above removes the weed. Only one thing removes the reason it was there: closing the gap. A crack filled with a sound, flexible sealant has no soil, no trapped moisture and no foothold for a seed. That is why professionals treat crack sealing as weed control as much as pavement maintenance, and why the same driveway can go from a weekly weeding chore to a non-issue in a single weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            The order matters. First, kill the weeds and give a systemic time to work if you used one, typically a week or two, so the roots die rather than regrow under the filler. Second, remove all dead plant material and dig the debris out of the crack, because sealing over roots and soil just traps the problem. Third, clean and dry the crack completely; most fillers will not bond to damp, dusty surfaces. Fourth, fill to the level the product directs, usually flush or just below the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to sealcoat an asphalt drive, do the cracks first. Our guide to whether you should <a href="/fill-driveway-cracks-before-sealing/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">fill driveway cracks before sealing</a> explains why sealer alone does not bridge cracks and why it peels over open ones. For pavers, refill the joints with polymeric sand after the weeds are cleared, and pick a product from our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best polymeric sand</a> tests that suits your joint width.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then keep the surroundings tidy. Most of what ends up in a driveway crack comes from the edge of the lawn and the leaves that pile against the slab. Trim the lawn edge where it meets the driveway so grass does not creep onto the pavement, which is a quick job with a sharp edge on a trimmer; our guide on <a href="/how-to-restring-a-weed-eater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to restring a weed eater</a> covers keeping the line fresh for that job. Blow leaves and grass clippings off the drive rather than letting them rot into the cracks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, inspect once a year, ideally in spring before seeds germinate. Look for new hairline cracks, sealant that has pulled away from the edges, and paver joints that have washed low. Ten minutes with a crack filler or a bag of joint sand at that stage prevents a summer of spraying. The best driveway weed killer, in the end, is a driveway with no open cracks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Live Weeds and Roots</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filler poured over a crack that still holds roots, soil and plant debris will not bond, and the weed simply pushes up beside it or lifts it out. Kill the weeds, wait for a systemic to finish working, dig the debris out, and fill only a clean, dry crack.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Salt on a Concrete Driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Salt kills crack weeds but soaks into the concrete and contributes to surface scaling, the same damage road salt causes, and it washes off the edge into the lawn. You trade a cosmetic weed problem for pavement repair and a dead strip of grass along the drive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying a Residual Near Tree Roots</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Year-long vegetation killers stay active in the soil and move with water. Roots from nearby trees and shrubs often run under the driveway and can take it up. Keep residual products away from drip lines, off slopes that drain into beds, and never apply them before rain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing Paver Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pressure washer clears debris from concrete cracks quickly, but on pavers it blasts the joint sand out and leaves deep, empty joints for the next round of weeds. Clean pavers with a wide fan tip held at a low angle, then refill the joints with polymeric sand rather than leaving them open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pulling the Tops and Leaving the Roots</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Snapping off a dandelion or yanking a handful of bindweed leaves feels productive, but the root regrows within weeks. Pull after rain when soil is soft, work the tool down beside the root, and treat perennials that keep returning with a method that reaches the roots.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best weed killer for driveway cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what you want. For perennial weeds that keep returning, a glyphosate product kills the roots. For bare cracks that stay bare for up to a year, a residual product such as Ortho GroundClear Year Long or Roundup Max Control 365 works, if no tree roots or beds are nearby. For no chemicals at all, use a flame weeder or boiling water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop weeds growing in driveway cracks permanently?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kill the existing weeds, remove the roots and debris from the crack, and then fill and seal it. Use a rubberized crack filler on asphalt, a flexible sealant on concrete joints, and polymeric sand between pavers. Without soil and moisture in the crack, seeds have nowhere to root. Inspect each spring and touch up new cracks before they fill with debris.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use Roundup on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Glyphosate products are labeled for driveways and patios, and they are reasonably safe when used exactly as the label directs. Spray on a dry, still day so it does not drift or wash off, keep children and pets away until it dries, and avoid overspray onto the lawn edge. Residual versions need extra care near trees and garden beds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does boiling water kill weeds in cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on contact. Boiling water kills the leaves and crown it touches and is most effective on young annual weeds. It does not reach deep roots, so perennials like dandelions usually regrow. Pour slowly and generously into the crack and plan to repeat in seven to ten days. It is best for a few cracks near the house rather than a whole driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use vinegar on pavers and concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but with care. Vinegar is acidic and reacts with the cement in concrete and concrete pavers, so repeated use can dull or etch the surface. It etches natural stone such as limestone and travertine quickly. Spray the weed rather than the paving, rinse overspray off with water, and do not rely on vinegar as a weekly routine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do year-long driveway weed killers last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Products such as Ortho GroundClear Year Long Vegetation Killer and Roundup Max Control 365 are labeled to prevent new growth for up to 12 months. Real-world results vary with rainfall, soil and how much debris sits in the cracks. Expect to retreat or spot-spray the following season, and fill the cracks so you need the product less often.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time to treat driveway weeds?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Treat when weeds are young and actively growing, usually spring and early summer, on a dry, calm day with no rain forecast for at least the time the label requires. Systemics work best on actively growing plants, and contact sprays work fastest in warm sun. Spring is also the ideal time to fill cracks before new seeds germinate.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

